update-node-alternatives for Debian
===================================

This package wraps update-alternatives(1) and the bundled nvm in order
to manage system-wide Node.js versions. A few packaging details are
worth recording so that future maintainers don't have to reverse them
out of the maintainer scripts.

Why dh_installalternatives is NOT used
--------------------------------------

dh_installalternatives expects the master link, slaves and target
paths to be known at packaging time, so it can generate a static
postinst that registers the alternative on every install. That model
does not fit this package: the targets live under

    /usr/local/share/nvm/versions/node/vX.Y.Z/bin/{node,npm,npx,corepack}

and that directory does not exist when the package is installed --
it is created later, at runtime, when the user runs
`update-node-alternatives --install <version>`. The version (and
therefore the path and priority) are also chosen by the user, not
known upfront.

The package therefore registers and removes alternatives at runtime,
from inside the wrapper script itself. The only static maintainer
script is debian/postrm, which calls

    update-alternatives --remove-all <name>

for node, npm, npx and corepack on `purge`, so that no dangling
entries stay behind.

postinst (debconf scenario 1.b)
-------------------------------

The package optionally offers to install a default Node.js version at
`apt install` time via debconf. The question and behaviour are
documented in debian/templates and debian/postinst:

  * latest LTS  -- runs `update-node-alternatives --install lts/*`
  * latest      -- runs `update-node-alternatives --install node`
  * none        -- skip; the user installs versions manually later
  * custom      -- ask for a specific version string

Under DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive the question is skipped (defaults
to "none"); no Node.js version is downloaded silently.

postrm (purge of /usr/local/share/nvm/)
---------------------------------------

The /usr/local/share/nvm/ store sits outside dpkg's tree (it lives
under /usr/local) and contains every Node.js version the admin has
ever installed. Wiping it on every `apt purge` would be surprising
and destructive, so the package stores the admin's preference at
install time (debian/templates: nvm-purge-on-purge) and re-confirms it
at purge time before deleting anything. Defaults to "no".

Maintainer scripts never write under /usr/local
-----------------------------------------------

Debian Policy 9.1.2 reserves /usr/local to the local administrator.
The alternatives links (/usr/local/bin/node, npm, ...) and the Node.js
store (/usr/local/share/nvm/) are only created or changed by the admin
running update-node-alternatives. That is why an upgrade that changes
the priority formula (0.4.2) does not re-register anything by itself:
debian/NEWS asks the admin to run `update-node-alternatives --update`.

Global npm packages (--install-packages, migration)
---------------------------------------------------

Global packages are reinstalled with the npm and node of the target
version (never copied), so native modules are rebuilt for its ABI.
Before installing, the engines.node range of each candidate is checked
with the semver module bundled with that version's npm; incompatible
packages start unchecked. npm then runs with --engine-strict, because
the version it fetches from the registry may differ from the local copy
that was checked.

Known deviations from Debian main
---------------------------------

The package targets a personal APT repository. For Debian main it
would still need to: unbundle nvm (Policy 4.13, embedded code copies;
debian/copyright documents vendor/nvm/), and drop or move to contrib
the optional debconf step that downloads a Node.js build from
nodejs.org at install time (default: none).
